# Android services stubbed out on sunfish because they only spam the journal
# under LuneOS/Halium (see meta-android's pre-start.d/50-stub-services).
#
# Derived from sargo's list rather than from a booted Pixel 4a: the two devices
# share the Pixel vendor stack (Titan M, pixelstats, the Qualcomm display
# post-processing daemon and diag loggers), and every entry below is named the
# same way in device/google/sunfish's init.hardware.rc or its vendor image.
# A path that does not exist on the device is simply never stubbed, so an
# entry that turns out to be wrong costs nothing - but nothing here has been
# confirmed against a running device yet.
#
# Pixel telemetry reporter; polls the never-present IStats framework service
# about once a second.
vendor/bin/pixelstats-vendor
# Waits for the Titan M strongbox keymaster, polling keymaster@3.0 hwservices
# that never register. init.hardware.rc exec_starts it in late-fs and *waits*,
# so the stub must exit or boot stops there.
vendor/bin/hw/wait_for_strongbox exit
# Titan M reboot-escrow HAL; polls the same keymaster services.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.rebootescrow-service.citadel
# Display post-processing daemon; needs the framework sensorservice for the
# ambient light sensor and crash-loops without it.
vendor/bin/mm-pp-dpps
# SurfaceFlinger configstore HAL. On sargo it died with SIGSYS inside
# ISurfaceFlingerConfigs::registerAsService() and init restarted it every five
# seconds - 235 coredumps and 30 MB in the first eight minutes. Stubbing it
# removes no capability: there is no surfaceflinger under Halium, and the
# libgui/libui clients fall back to their compiled-in defaults.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.configstore@1.1-service
# On-device modem/diag loggers. diag_mdlog holds DIAG_WS and the cdsp/lpass
# IPC-router ports awake and was the measured suspend aborter on sargo. init
# exec-starts it as a oneshot, so the stub must exit rather than sleep.
vendor/bin/diag_mdlog exit
vendor/bin/ssr_diag
# storaged, which polls hwservicemanager once a second for the battery health
# HAL. Nothing in LuneOS consumes either (batteryd and nyx read sysfs).
system/bin/storaged
#
# The health HAL itself is deliberately NOT stubbed, though sargo's list stubs
# its 2.0 equivalent. Stubbing it here cost more noise than it saved: on
# 22 Sep 2026 it produced 621 lines of
#
#   init: Control message: Could not find
#         'android.hardware.health@2.1::IHealth/default' for ctl.interface_start
#         from pid: 39 (/system/system_ext/bin/hwservicemanager)
#
# in one ten-minute boot, plus 26 lazy-start attempts, because storaged is not
# the only client. The one that keeps asking is gnss_service - identified by
# pid: it is the process logging "HidlServiceManagement: Waited one second for
# android.hardware.health@2.1::IHealth/default", once a second, forever - and
# GPS is something LuneOS does want, so silencing the client is the wrong end.
#
# Note the vendor's own rc declares the service with no "interface" line:
#
#   service health-hal-2-1 /vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.health@2.1-service
#       class hal charger
#
# so init cannot resolve a ctl.interface_start for it no matter what, and the
# only way the request is ever satisfied is the real HAL registering IHealth on
# its own. It reads /sys/class/power_supply, which works here (google_battery
# and google_charger are in-kernel and healthy), so let it run.
#
# If it does misbehave on hardware, note it holds WAKE_ALARM and BLOCK_SUSPEND
# for its periodic battery chores - the class of thing that made diag_mdlog the
# measured suspend aborter on sargo. Re-stub it then and accept the spam.
# USB gadget HAL. LuneOS owns the USB gadget on the host side (its own configfs
# gadget for adb/rndis, driven by usb-gadget.target), so the container never
# gets a /config/usb_gadget/g1 - and this HAL's constructor treats that as
# fatal. Confirmed on the device, 22 Sep 2026: 125 starts and 124 SIGABRTs in
# ten minutes, the backtrace one frame deep in the constructor
#
#   #01 pc 43e0  UsbGadget::UsbGadget()+116
#   #02 pc 420c  main+40
#
# and the binary carries the string that goes with it, "configfs setup not done
# yet", next to the /config/usb_gadget/g1/os_desc paths it checks. Every crash
# also sets sys.init.updatable_crashing=1, which ran flags_health_check 269
# times in the same window, so this is noisy well beyond its own service.
# class hal, restart-on-death - so the sleeping stub, not the exiting one.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.usb.gadget-service.sunfish
